-0458cbfae2b2"] ["question-f3f7c8b0-18ee-5d7c-bcd5-63f6de1aef9a" :question/id #uuid "f3f7c8b0-18ee-5d7c-bcd5-63f6de1aef9a"] ["question-f3f7c8b0-18ee-5d7c-bcd5-63f6de1aef9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-108296"] ["question-f3f7c8b0-18ee-5d7c-bcd5-63f6de1aef9a" :question/problem "![](images/e0/e0b8b8968b650e53d35766ede2b22aa187a7301f559d1687fa6bc630c1152701.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x)$ attains a local minimum at $x=-2$\n\nII. $f(x)$ attains a **global** minimum at $x=1$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ attains a local maximum at $x=3$"] ["question-f3f7c8b0-18ee-5d7c-bcd5-63f6de1aef9a" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function has a local minimum at $x=-2.$ For example, in the interval $(-2.5,-1.5),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=-2,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/f7/f7abc15ac37d26d6daea9855cdebd2ef0ae5e27a27d65480b51da3cb7b19a395.png)\n\n \nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\ge f(-2)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (-2.5,-1.5).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the function has a global minimum at $x=1.$ The global minimum is the lowest point on the graph and our function does not have points where its value is smaller than $f(1).$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=3.$ For example, in the interval $(2.5,3.5),$ the greatest value of the function is attained at $x=3,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/19/19d3370f7113e6ebfaa2d1f53a3d097de796e6d3413f06414d35e6a7ade9b9b6.png)\n\n \nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\le f(3)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (2.5,3.5).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] ["question-f3f7c8b0-18ee-5d7c-bcd5-63f6de1aef9a" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-1a5d9368-bd94-5aac-9c74-f801a63761ce" :answer-field/id #uuid "1a5d9368-bd94-5aac-9c74-f801a63761ce"] ["answer-58b3937e-8748-534e-8f8a-8a78179a3ca0" :answer/id #uuid "58b3937e-8748-534e-8f8a-8a78179a3ca0"] ["answer-58b3937e-8748-534e-8f8a-8a78179a3ca0" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-58b3937e-8748-534e-8f8a-8a78179a3ca0" :answer/value "I only"] ["answer-cd6d6a7c-e4ba-5e33-ad17-2e914a1c904b" :answer/id #uuid "cd6d6a7c-e4ba-5e33-ad17-2e914a1c904b"] ["answer-cd6d6a7c-e4ba-5e33-ad17-2e914a1c904b" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-cd6d6a7c-e4ba-5e33-ad17-2e914a1c904b" :answer/value "I, II, and III"] ["answer-f922dea5-ab43-5da8-94b1-11fd66e881c6" :answer/id #uuid "f922dea5-ab43-5da8-94b1-11fd66e881c6"] ["answer-f922dea5-ab43-5da8-94b1-11fd66e881c6" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-f922dea5-ab43-5da8-94b1-11fd66e881c6" :answer/value "II and III only"] ["answer-3e2ebe3b-5917-5d4e-b709-6ae7dc69f409" :answer/id #uuid "3e2ebe3b-5917-5d4e-b709-6ae7dc69f409"] ["answer-3e2ebe3b-5917-5d4e-b709-6ae7dc69f409" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-3e2ebe3b-5917-5d4e-b709-6ae7dc69f409" :answer/value "II only"] ["answer-8b1acef0-82ab-5835-b335-a0df696d5895" :answer/id #uuid "8b1acef0-82ab-5835-b335-a0df696d5895"] ["answer-8b1acef0-82ab-5835-b335-a0df696d5895" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-8b1acef0-82ab-5835-b335-a0df696d5895" :answer/value "III only"] ["answer-field-1a5d9368-bd94-5aac-9c74-f801a63761ce" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-1a5d9368-bd94-5aac-9c74-f801a63761ce" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-1a5d9368-bd94-5aac-9c74-f801a63761ce" :answer-field/correct "answer-cd6d6a7c-e4ba-5e33-ad17-2e914a1c904b"] ["answer-field-1a5d9368-bd94-5aac-9c74-f801a63761ce" :answer-field/choices "answer-58b3937e-8748-534e-8f8a-8a78179a3ca0"] ["answer-field-1a5d9368-bd94-5aac-9c74-f801a63761ce" :answer-field/choices "answer-cd6d6a7c-e4ba-5e33-ad17-2e914a1c904b"] ["answer-field-1a5d9368-bd94-5aac-9c74-f801a63761ce" :answer-field/choices "answer-f922dea5-ab43-5da8-94b1-11fd66e881c6"] ["answer-field-1a5d9368-bd94-5aac-9c74-f801a63761ce" :answer-field/choices "answer-3e2ebe3b-5917-5d4e-b709-6ae7dc69f409"] ["answer-field-1a5d9368-bd94-5aac-9c74-f801a63761ce" :answer-field/choices "answer-8b1acef0-82ab-5835-b335-a0df696d5895"] ["question-f3f7c8b0-18ee-5d7c-bcd5-63f6de1aef9a" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-1a5d9368-bd94-5aac-9c74-f801a63761ce"] [17592186152218 :knowledge-point/questions "question-f3f7c8b0-18ee-5d7c-bcd5-63f6de1aef9a"] [17592186153362 :tutorial/content "A **local extremum** (or **relative extremum**) of a function is a point that is either a maximum or minimum point of the function relative to an (open) interval in the function's domain. \n\nFor example, consider the function shown in the graph below. \n\n![](images/19/197834de138cb2e05a3af391bbeec1279ad8bfddfb277b881e5e3eeb49bcb73a.png)\n\nThe graph has a peak at the point $A$ where $x=0.$ We say that the function has a **local maximum** when $x=0.$ We also say that the point $A$ is a **local maximum point** of the function. \n\nThis means that we can take an open interval of $x$-values around $x=0$ such that $A$ will be the highest point on the graph in this interval. \n\n![](images/d5/d522b4b0a59ff328399493bc12a770af2d99a1587b2826accb9835d7463ef9ba.png)\n\nSimilarly, the graph has a valley at the point $B$ where $x=2.$ We say that the function has a **local minimum** when $x=2.$ We can also say that the point $B$ is a **local minimum point** of the function.\n\nThis means that we can take an open interval of $x$-values around $x=2$ such that $B$ will be the lowest point on the graph in this interval. \n\n![](images/e2/e2a92d4217e1eebcc724e48cca273d2e707731ded914207e59fff149b46b4dcc.png)"] [17592186153407 :tutorial/content "Let's go back to looking at the function $y=f(x)$ that we saw earlier.\n\n![](images/19/197834de138cb2e05a3af391bbeec1279ad8bfddfb277b881e5e3eeb49bcb73a.png)\n\nThe point $A$ where $x=0$ is a local maximum of $f(x)$ because we can draw an open interval around $x=0$ such that the point $A$ is the highest point on the graph. One such interval is $x\\in (-0.5,0.5),$ shown below:\n\n![](images/d5/d522b4b0a59ff328399493bc12a770af2d99a1587b2826accb9835d7463ef9ba.png)\n\nEverywhere in this interval, the value of the function is *less than or equal to* $f(0),$ the largest value of the function in this interval. We can write this mathematically as\n\n$\\qquad$ $f(x) \\leq f(0)$ for all $x\\in(-0.5, 0.5).$\n\nSimilarly, the point $B$ where $x=2$ is a local minimum of $f(x)$ because we can draw an open interval around $x=2$ such that the point $B$ is the lowest point on the graph. One such interval is $x\\in (1.5,2.5),$ shown below:\n\n![](images/e2/e2a92d4217e1eebcc724e48cca273d2e707731ded914207e59fff149b46b4dcc.png)\n\nEverywhere in this interval, the value of the function is *greater than or equal to* $f(2),$ the smallest value of the function in this interval. We can write this mathematically as\n\n$\\qquad$ $f(x) \\geq f(2)$ for all $x\\in(1.5, 2.5).$"] [17592186153406 :tutorial/content "Can a point on a graph be both a local minimum *and* a local maximum?\n\nTo answer this question, let's consider the function $f(x)$ given by the graph below. \n\n![](images/0c/0cec91d9ec8e108ee4836e816aae63722fc4c09d997790eaf283cd3e2a9c4bc6.png)\n\nNow consider the point where $x=2.$ Our goal is to determine which type of extremum it is. To do this, we consider the values of $f(x)$ in the open interval $(1.5, 2.5),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/c6/c6cf1a6237c50d5c2b651937ca46b9ae52a08dafb831d76b27ddb068d37dd501.png)\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- The point where $x=2$ is a local maximum because $f(x) \\le f(2) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (1.5,2.5)\\quad {\\color{\\green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- The point where $x=2$ is a local minimum because $f(x) \\ge f(2) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (1.5,2.5)\\quad {\\color{\\green}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is both a local maximum *and* a local minimum of $f(x)!$ \n\nThis might seem strange. But, notice that $x=2$ lies within an interval where the function is constant. This is a special case where a particular point is both a local minimum and a local maximum."] [17592186152350 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0]] "retractions" [[17592186151973 :question/worked-solution "Consider the point $U$. We can take an open interval of $x$-values such that $U$ will be the highest point on the graph in this interval. So, $U$ is a local maximum.\n\nA similar interval exists for the point $W$, too. So, $W$ is also a local maximum.\n\n![](images/ac/ac0a6e28e4564883e6743b2d600cb8611723e813e85c1acb5fcc327ee3dbc9b5.png)"] [17592186152026 :question/problem "For which of the following values of $x$ does the function $y = f(x)$, shown below, attain a local minimum?\n\n1. $x=0$\n2. $x=1$\n3. $x=3$\n\n![](images/17/1718b48d39255b221037a439cc48d300a04c33ffb4e04c700fefb303a20ebff2.png)"] [17592186152026 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given points in turn.\n\n- The function has a local minimum at $x=0$. For example, in the interval $(-0.5,0.5)$, the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=0$, as shown below.![](images/e2/e25e6e31d6ea5d7f882d4eed16c82ec8280d1ed56f275974e8f07091c0e62c5d.png)\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x) \\ge f(0) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (-0.5,0.5)$.\n- The function does *not* have a local minimum at $x=1$. For any open interval containing $x=1$, there will be points on the graph that lie below $f(1)$.\n\n- The function has a local minimum at $x=3$. For example, in the interval $(2.5,3.5)$, the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=3$, as shown below.![](images/d1/d133d7ee4c022b47740f62b8e73ba9ea2f70e788861c25d2824b5e7236027c60.png)\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x) \\ge f(3) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (2.5,3.5)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186152027 :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the function shown below. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f(x)$ attains a local maximum at $x=-2$\n2. $f(x)$ attains a local minimum at $x=2$\n3. $f(x)$ attains a local maximum at $x=0$\n\n![](images/9b/9b933b1b869b74f2a7340f9ba4034b970c49434d12592d3ff1e152057b6adef1.png)"] [17592186152027 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function has a local minimum at $x=-2$. For example, in the interval $(-2.5,-1.5)$, the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=-2$, as shown below.![](images/3a/3abb7470db51fa1bdb61e15aec1739de44bf5083b7341b90a65ac93fc7f8adbb.png)\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x) \\ge f(-2) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (-2.5,-1.5)$.\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a local minimum at $x=2$. For example, in the interval $(1.5,2.5)$, the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=2$, as shown below.![](images/71/71c6e7af0a1de77543b57d2aee242fe84295ad46c748b2f65f7cba381c47b80c.png)\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x) \\ge f(2) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (1.5,2.5)$.\n\n- Statement III is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=0$. For example, in the interval $(-0.5,0.5)$, the greatest value of the function is attained at $x=0$, as shown below.![](images/83/83de300c3e00766d46989d12b4e1708ef9a73f4d02748009a526b1646d7f0b81.png)\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x) \\le f(0) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (-0.5,0.5)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186152028 :question/problem "The graph of a function $y = f(x)$ is shown below. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f(x)$ attains a local minimum at $x=2$\n2. $f(x)$ attains a **global** maximum at $x=0$\n3. $f(x)$ attains a local minimum at $x=-2$\n\n![](images/91/91d9d4997dd58c2c3cb07efc1529487bbd9a5a2f5c611054289cfc2ba24d764c.png)"] [17592186152028 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function has a local minimum at $x=2$. For example, in the interval $(1.5,2.5)$, the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=2$, as shown below.![](images/00/00a21a670b2080ce766ddf142eb2f37303d7af7b667e1b5038eee93879c071f7.png)\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x) \\ge f(2) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (1.5,2.5)$.\n- Statement II is false. The function does *not* have a global maximum at $x=0$. The global maximum is the highest point on the graph, but our function has points where its value is greater than $f(0)$. For example, $f(0) \\lt f(3)$.\n\n- Statement III is true. The function has a local minimum at $x=-2$. For example, in the interval $(-2.5,-1.5)$, the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=-2$, as shown below.![](images/fc/fc96fe5bb938b0ca8569b864588348a4d22280e3c4a5806cdd20af6eb4bcea34.png)\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x) \\ge f(-2) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (-2.5,-1.5)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186152589 :question/problem "![](images/20/20b6e19fcb7e1fe4f33697d517c28d9563c793634cfbcd5552d842efeef3ef61.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y = f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are the local maxima of the function?"] [17592186150041 :answer-field/choices 17592186150046] [17592186152594 :question/problem "![](images/c4/c46e88b9e044fccb91e0efa4ee981973a286e0335f58a70f7b1f9a856d9bfed4.png)\n\nFor which of the following values of $x$ does the function $y = f(x)$, shown above, attain a local minimum?\n\n1. $x = 3$\n2. $x = 2$\n3. $x =-2$"] [17592186153362 :tutorial/content "A **local extremum** (or **relative extremum**) of a function is a point that is either a maximum or minimum point of the function relative to an (open) interval in the function's domain.\n\nFor example, consider the function shown in the graph below.\n\n![](images/19/197834de138cb2e05a3af391bbeec1279ad8bfddfb277b881e5e3eeb49bcb73a.png)\n\nThe graph has a peak at the point $A$ where $x=0$. We say that the function has a **local maximum** when $x=0$. We also say that the point $A$ is a **local maximum point** of the function.\n\nThis means that we can take an open interval of $x$-values around $x=0$ such that $A$ will be the highest point on the graph in this interval.\n\n![](images/d5/d522b4b0a59ff328399493bc12a770af2d99a1587b2826accb9835d7463ef9ba.png)\n\nSimilarly, the graph has a valley at the point $B$ where $x=2$. We say that the function has a **local minimum** when $x=2$. We can also say that the point $B$ is a **local minimum point** of the function.\n\nThis means that we can take an open interval of $x$-values around $x=2$ such that $B$ will be the lowest point on the graph in this interval.\n\n![](images/e2/e2a92d4217e1eebcc724e48cca273d2e707731ded914207e59fff149b46b4dcc.png)"] [17592186153407 :tutorial/content "Let's go back to looking at the function $y=f(x)$ that we saw earlier.\n\n![](images/19/197834de138cb2e05a3af391bbeec1279ad8bfddfb277b881e5e3eeb49bcb73a.png)\n\nThe point $A$ where $x=0$ is a local maximum of $f(x)$ because we can draw an open interval around $x=0$ such that the point $A$ is the highest point on the graph. One such interval is $x\\in (-0.5,0.5)$, shown below:\n\n![](images/d5/d522b4b0a59ff328399493bc12a770af2d99a1587b2826accb9835d7463ef9ba.png)\n\nEverywhere in this interval, the value of the function is *less than or equal to* $f(0)$, the largest value of the function in this interval. We can write this mathematically as\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\nf(x) \\leq f(0)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in(-0.5, 0.5)$.\n\nSimilarly, the point $B$ where $x=2$ is a local minimum of $f(x)$ because we can draw an open interval around $x=2$ such that the point $B$ is the lowest point on the graph. One such interval is $x\\in (1.5,2.5)$, shown below:\n\n![](images/e2/e2a92d4217e1eebcc724e48cca273d2e707731ded914207e59fff149b46b4dcc.png)\n\nEverywhere in this interval, the value of the function is *greater than or equal to* $f(2)$, the smallest value of the function in this interval. We can write this mathematically as\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\nf(x) \\geq f(2)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in(1.5, 2.5)$."] [17592186153406 :tutorial/content "Can a point on a graph be both a local minimum *and* a local maximum?\n\nTo answer this question, let's consider the function $f(x)$ given by the graph below.\n\n![](images/0c/0cec91d9ec8e108ee4836e816aae63722fc4c09d997790eaf283cd3e2a9c4bc6.png)\n\nNow consider the point where $x=2$. Our goal is to determine which type of extremum it is. To do this, we consider the values of $f(x)$ in the open interval $(1.5, 2.5)$, shown below.\n\n![](images/c6/c6cf1a6237c50d5c2b651937ca46b9ae52a08dafb831d76b27ddb068d37dd501.png)\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- The point where $x=2$ is a local maximum because $f(x) \\le f(2) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (1.5,2.5)\\quad {\\color{\\green}\\checkmark}$\n- The point where $x=2$ is a local minimum because $f(x) \\ge f(2) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (1.5,2.5)\\quad {\\color{\\green}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is both a local maximum *and* a local minimum of $f(x)$!\n\nThis might seem strange. But, notice that $x=2$ lies within an interval where the function is constant. This is a special case where a particular point is both a local minimum and a local maximum."]]}]